\section{renv profiles}{

An renv profile is a named set of packages with a lockfile of its own.
\code{write_dockerfile()} picks the profile by the rule of renv 1.3.1, which
\code{check_supplement(renv_lock = TRUE)} also uses. It does not read
\code{RENV_PATHS_LOCKFILE} or \code{RENV_PATHS_RENV}. If the \code{RENV_PROFILE}
environment variable of the R session that runs \code{write_dockerfile()} is
not empty, it names the profile. Otherwise the first line of
\code{renv/profile} in the site folder names it, when that file exists. The
name \code{default}, an empty name, or no name means no profile, and the
lockfile is \code{renv.lock} in the site folder. With a profile, the lockfile
is \verb{renv/profiles/<name>/renv.lock}, and the function prints a message
that names the lockfile, the profile, and where the name came from.

With a profile, the \code{.dockerignore} lets that lockfile into the image.
The \code{Dockerfile} copies it to the same path and sets the \code{RENV_PROFILE}
environment variable of the image to the name. renv keeps the packages
of a profile in a library of their own, so the build restores them there,
and each render in the image uses the profile, whatever \code{renv/profile} in
the mounted site folder names. The name must hold only ASCII letters,
digits, \code{.}, \verb{_}, and \code{-}, and must not start with \code{.}, so that the
\code{Dockerfile} can hold it. Each folder and file in the path
\verb{renv/profiles/<name>/renv.lock} must have that letter case, because
Docker matches file names with letter case.
}

\section{Build and render}{

In a terminal, in the site folder, build the image and give it a name:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker build -t my-supplement .
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

Then render one report in it with the site folder mounted at \verb{/site}.
On macOS and Linux:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker run --rm --network none --user "$(id -u):$(id -g)" -e HOME=/tmp \\
  -v "$PWD":/site my-supplement quarto render analyses/example.qmd
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\verb{--network none} shows that the render needs nothing from the network.
\code{--user} runs the render as you. On Linux it makes you the owner of the
files that the render writes into the site folder; Docker Desktop on
macOS does that without it. \verb{HOME=/tmp} gives that user a home folder in
the container.
}

\section{Versions that can change}{

An image tag such as \verb{rocker/r-ver:4.6.1} can be moved to a newer image,
so without \code{digest}, a later build can get newer system libraries. To
pin the base image, read the digest of its multi-platform index:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker buildx imagetools inspect rocker/r-ver:4.6.1
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

and pass the \verb{Digest:} value, such as \code{"sha256:bafb..."}, as \code{digest}.
The build also installs Ubuntu packages for the R packages. These come
from Ubuntu's package archive at build time, so they can be newer in a
later build even with \code{digest}.

To keep the built image itself, write it to a file that you can archive:

\if{html}{\out{<div class="sourceCode">}}\preformatted{docker save -o my-supplement.tar my-supplement
}\if{html}{\out{</div>}}

\verb{docker load -i my-supplement.tar} reads it back. Commit the
\code{Dockerfile} and the \code{.dockerignore}: the Zenodo archive of a GitHub
release then holds them, but not the image.
}

\section{Errors}{

The function checks the arguments, \code{renv/profile}, \code{renv.lock}, and both target paths
before it writes a file, so after any error but a failed write the site
folder is as it was. It writes \code{.dockerignore} first, then the
\code{Dockerfile}. A failed write, an open that fails included, can leave
\code{.dockerignore} written, and either file empty or cut short. A file that
cannot be opened for writing gives R's own error. The other errors have
the class \code{supplement_error} and one of these classes:
\itemize{
\item \code{supplement_error_argument}: \code{path} is not a single non-missing,
non-empty string, or it is not a folder. \code{quarto} is missing or is not
one string of three whole numbers joined by \code{.}, each \code{0} or without
a leading zero. \code{digest} is not \code{NULL} or one string of \verb{sha256:} and
64 lowercase hex digits. \code{overwrite} is not \code{TRUE} or \code{FALSE}.
\item \code{supplement_error_profile}: \code{renv/profile} is read and is a folder or
a file that cannot be opened, or the profile name holds a character
other than an ASCII letter, a digit, \code{.}, \verb{_}, or \code{-}, or starts with
\code{.}. The name check comes before any check of a lockfile.
\item \code{supplement_error_no_file}: the folder has no \code{renv.lock}, or
\code{renv.lock} is a folder or a link to a missing file. With a profile,
this is also the error when a folder or file in the path
\verb{renv/profiles/<name>/renv.lock} has another letter case.
\item \code{supplement_error_lockfile}: \code{renv.lock} is not a JSON object, has no
\code{R} object with a \code{Version}, or has no \code{renv} entry with a \code{Version}
under \code{Packages}, or one of the two versions is not one string of three
whole numbers joined by \code{.}. A development version such as
\verb{1.1.5.9000} is such an error, because the build installs released
versions. An R version older than 4.0.0 or a renv version older than
1.3.1 is also this error. So is a key that the function reads, given
twice in the same object: \code{R} or \code{Packages} at the top level,
\code{Version} in \code{R}, \code{renv} in \code{Packages}, or \code{Version} in that \code{renv}
entry. This check comes before the version checks.
\item \code{supplement_error_file_exists}: \code{Dockerfile} or \code{.dockerignore}
exists and \code{overwrite} is \code{FALSE}. A folder at either path is this
error even when \code{overwrite} is \code{TRUE}. So is a link, also a link to a
missing file, because a write would follow it.
